# Manuscript PDF page 21

                         T H E C O I N R U N S WA R M



   I looked up at the third-floor window. Dark. I thanked him like a
normal person and walked to the train with my heartbeat doing some‐
thing rude.



The café sits under the El on Jerome, so twice every few minutes the
whole place shakes and everyone keeps talking like the building isn’t
trying to leave. The sign out front says EL DISCO CAFÉ, and if you
look at the paint above it, the older letters show through: DISCOS
QUISQUEYA. The room is narrow and loud, with a tin ceiling, mis‐
matched chairs, and a back wall where the record bins used to run.
    Mr. Rodríguez owns the café the way a cat owns a house. He’s also a
poet, which means in practice that nothing in the building is fully fin‐
ished, including him.
    “Dime a ver, poeta,” he said when I came in, not looking up from a
tray. “And that face.”
    “What face?”
    “The one you’re wearing. Like you’re listening to a station the rest of
us don’t get.” He set the tray down and finally looked at me. He has his
father’s eyebrows, I found out later, but all I knew then was that they
could say a paragraph by themselves. “Bad night?”
    “Long night. The power went out.”
    “The whole block, sí. Doña Fela says the Lord.” He wiped his hands.
“Go on. Tuesday. Three gigs, no lunch. I’m not paying you to look
haunted. Though it is good for the brand.”
    Tuesday was gig one of three. Gig two was the mural downstairs,
which I’d broken on purpose at 3:07. Gig three was the beat-mixing for
a kid in Soundview who wanted his EP to sound like it cost more than
it did, which I’d finished a week ago and could not make myself send.
Rent was due in nine days. I do this math constantly, as a background
process I can’t kill: if the café pays Friday, and the mural milestone
clears, and the kid pays his half. It’s not accounting. It’s tigueraje, the
old neighborhood skill of getting through the month on nerve and
arithmetic.




